<article class="env-lemma" id="00FT">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00FT">Lemma <span data-tag="00FT">10.35.10</span>.</a> Suppose that $k$ is a field and suppose that $V$ is a nonzero vector space over $k$. Assume the dimension of $V$ (which is a cardinal number) is smaller than the cardinality of $k$. Then for any linear operator $T : V \to V$ there exists some monic polynomial $P(t) \in k[t]$ such that $P(T)$ is not invertible. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If not then $V$ inherits the structure of a vector space over the field $k(t)$. But the dimension of $k(t)$ over $k$ is at least the cardinality of $k$ for example due to the fact that the elements $\frac{1}{t - \lambda }$ are $k$-linearly independent. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>